[[ch02-introduction]]

What expectations should we have when thinking about either improving government or replacing government in 2026? This introduction advocates for control not freedom as the driving force and expectation for everyone.

The American founders really messed up framing the government as freedom based. It was such a terrible mistake.

"And freedom, oh, freedom, well, that's just some people talkin'
Your prison is walkin' through this world all alone."
-The Eagles Desperado

"Freedom's just another word for nothin' left to lose."
-Janis Joplin, Me and Bobby McGee

"free as in speech or free as in beer?"
-Open Source Software debate between unfettered versus no cost software

Are people promoting socialism promoting freedom where governments provide services that are unfettered and of no cost to the individual?

Does freedom mean the capability to live unfettered from laws?

Does freedom mean some unfettered tug-of-war of control as with absolute free speech advocates empowering bullies?

Framing Democracy as built on freedom and its child rights was wrong-headed and irresponsible.

We live in tyranny and oppression today. We do not live in freedom here in the U.S. The only freedom we have is propaganda. For example, when we look for jobs then we are not free to walk into the company of our choice and demand to be hired. That's freedom. No, instead our freedom is a selection of tyrannical organizations to abuse us. One is at the mercy of institutions and corporations that will have absolute power over hiring you and the rules you must follow when hired. You have no say in the rules. That's not freedom. That's tyranny. A choice of tyranny is not freedom.

Our expectations of any future government change should be founded on control. Personal control. We should be able to control or choose who hires us and determine the rules of the place of employment once hired. Anything less is tyranny and oppression. Today "freedom" is simply choosing the tyranny we "want", and in many cases accepting any tyranny just to put food on the table.

We do not live in freedom. We live in tyranny.

The correct framing of government is control and responsibility. So our expectations should be to discard the ideas of freedom and rights in favor of control and responsibilities.

What we want is control, but we need responsibilities. We need responsibilities to institute mutual, shared benefits. High tech is a product of millions of people laboring to produce our smart phones and now AI. We depend on society for society's benefits and therefore personal control must be balanced with society controlling us.

The First Law of Control is that we must perceive to balance the individual versus society with both being of equal importance. Anything else and society would crush us. Anything less would relieve us of our social obligations.

For example, I currently perceive the social importance of renewable energy to mean I should drive an electric vehicle. This is my opinion. However, I own a gas car that is fifteen years old. My budget is such that buying a new car is detrimental to my financial health. Should I weigh society's needs as more important than my own or my own first? The First Law of Control says they have equal weight. Therefore I'm "right" or "moral" with either decision. My life in my personal scheme has to be as important as all of society in order for me to believe I'm a valuable person. The choice of tipping the decision scale is mine. I'm currently choosing to keep my gas powered car with the caveat that I am constantly keeping up with elective vehicle news in order to know when the financial situation becomes more favorable.

We have responsibilities to the society that provides for us. Conversely, society needs us to feel important in order to have a valuable life that justifies society's very services and existence.

Control then is a constant, changing conversation with every individual about obligations to society and oneself.

What we want is personal control balanced with social responsibilities. Some people may sacrifice personal control by joining the military and even dying for us. Other people, the rich, want no social responsibilities and only to control the rest of us with tyranny and oppression.

So, evaluate New Democracy with this new lens to evaluate our existing society and governments. Do you have personal control offset by a proper amount of social responsibilities? Freedoms and rights are unfettered propositions, unfettered from social responsibilities. So it is time to end conversations about freedom and rights. It is time to begin conversations about control and responsibilities.

However, personal control and social responsibilities are not our only primary concerns when thinking about social improvements because control is not passion driven in human nature, unfortunately.

People adjust to whatever control they are allowed without thinking. This is why citizens of extremely oppressive countries do not rise up to overthrow the government like in Iran today and Iraq twenty-five years ago. It is also why Americans are not rising up against their own government today.

Control is not a passion for most people. This is why historically propaganda must be used to stoke the fires within people to fight for freedom.Give me liberty or give me death! 

So, this means we need three cornerstones for judging any new government or social proposal and not just the two of personal control and social responsibilities.

The third cornerstone is to replace top-down control with cooperative control.  Any new system must destroy hierarchy, patriarchy, capitalism, and all other systems that have top-down control that benefits the few or the one over the many. 

All of our organizations today are built on bureaucracy built on patriarchy built on hierarchy. These must be destroyed. New Democracy is a mesh design. Mesh is a design for cooperative control and not top-down control. So, our third cornerstone for any new government change or replacement must be a system to eliminate top-down control and replace it with cooperative control.

Personal control, social responsibilities, and cooperative social control are the new values to judge any changes to society today. They are our new cornerstones.


